---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import AnchorPlayground from "../../components/Blog/AnchorPlayground.astro"
import { Anchor, AnchorContent, Button } from "opui-css/astro"
import Position from "../../component-examples/tooltip/Position.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="tooltip-interest-invokers">
  <section id="the-problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A tooltip opens on hover and focus, closes on <kbd>Esc</kbd>, blur and
      leave, and stays next to its trigger without running off the screen. Every
      part of that used to be JavaScript: event listeners, delays and a
      positioning library that measures and flips.
    </p>
    <p>Now it's two attributes and a few lines of CSS.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-trick">
    <h2>The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>interestfor</code> on the trigger and <code>popover="hint"</code> on
        the tooltip: it opens on hover and focus, no JavaScript
      </li>
      <li>On a touch screen, a long press shows it</li>
      <li>
        The invoker becomes the implicit anchor: no <code>anchor-name</code>, no
        ids to wire up in CSS
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>position-area: self-block-start</code> centers it above the trigger.
        The <code>self-</code> keywords read the tooltip's own writing mode, so a
        vertical tooltip or one in a right-to-left page still sits on its block start
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="html"
      code={`<button
  type="button"
  interestfor="tooltip"
>
  Save
</button>

<span class="tooltip" id="tooltip" popover="hint">Save changes</span>`}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="staying-on-screen">
    <h2>Staying on screen</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>position-try-fallbacks</code> flips the tooltip below the trigger when
        there's no room above
      </li>
      <li>
        Two <code>@position-try</code> rules use <code>anchor-size(inline)</code
        > to slide it sideways at the edge of the window, still over the trigger
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>position-visibility: anchors-visible</code> hides it when the trigger
        scrolls out of view
      </li>
      <li>
        An arrow follows along: the tooltip is its own anchor, and <code
          >clamp()</code
        > keeps the arrow on its edge, as close to the trigger as it gets
      </li>
      <li>
        Near an edge, <code>calc-size(max-content, min(size, 10rem))</code> keeps
        long text at least <code>10rem</code> wide, while short labels stay snug
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="try-it">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Drag the button around, or move it with the sliders. The tooltip stays
      open here so you can watch it: at the top of the box it drops below the
      button, at a side it shifts along it.
    </p>
    <Example wide showCode={false}>
      <AnchorPlayground>
        <Anchor class="ui-tooltip" positionArea="block-start">
          <Button>Move me</Button>
          <AnchorContent>Still on screen</AnchorContent>
        </Anchor>
      </AnchorPlayground>
    </Example>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>position-try-fallbacks</code> tries <code>flip-inline</code> and
        <code>flip-block</code> first, then the two <code>@position-try</code>
        options that shift the tooltip along the button
      </li>
      <li>
        Once a fallback fits, the browser keeps it until it stops fitting, so
        the tooltip doesn't jump back and forth as you move the button
      </li>
      <li>
        This tooltip is the always-visible variant, positioned inside the box.
        The real one is a <code>popover</code> in the top layer, so it keeps itself
        inside the window instead
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hover or focus a button. Scroll it near an edge and the tooltip moves to
      stay on screen.
    </p>
    <Example row spacious showCode={false}>
      <Position />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section id="fallback">
    <h2>Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without interest invokers, the compat layer shows the tooltip with <code
          >:has(&gt; :hover)</code
        > and <code>:has(&gt; :focus-visible)</code> on its wrapper, inside <code
          >@supports not selector(:interest-source)</code
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        There it isn't in the top layer, so an ancestor that clips its overflow
        can hide it, and touch screens get nothing. A toggletip, a button whose
        only job is to show the text, adds <code>commandfor</code> with <code
          >command="toggle-popover"</code
        > for a tap
      </li>
      <li>
        There's no <code>@supports</code> fallback for anchor positioning or <code
          >calc-size()</code
        >: those declarations are ignored, and the tooltip isn't placed next to
        its trigger
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
